1
00:00:00,378 --> 00:00:03,347
Meet Wrinkles, a naked mole-rat worker.

2
00:00:03,347 --> 00:00:07,032
Her colony is a maze of chambers joined by tunnels.

3
00:00:07,032 --> 00:00:10,678
Mathematicians draw a maze like this as a graph.

4
00:00:10,678 --> 00:00:13,777
Each chamber is a dot, called a vertex.

5
00:00:13,777 --> 00:00:16,533
Each tunnel is a line, called an edge.

6
00:00:17,428 --> 00:00:20,488
Wrinkles is in the nest, vertex S.

7
00:00:20,488 --> 00:00:25,605
Her job is to find every chamber she can reach by following tunnels.

8
00:00:25,605 --> 00:00:28,652
That question has a name: reachability.

9
00:00:28,652 --> 00:00:31,751
And one burrow over here was cut off by a cave-in,

10
00:00:31,751 --> 00:00:33,444
so she will never reach it.

11
00:00:33,444 --> 00:00:35,019
Pause, and think.

12
00:00:35,019 --> 00:00:37,646
How would Wrinkles check every chamber?

13
00:00:43,571 --> 00:00:46,839
Wrinkles' first idea: follow any tunnel.

14
00:00:46,839 --> 00:00:49,977
At the next chamber, follow any tunnel again.

15
00:00:49,977 --> 00:00:50,967
Watch.

16
00:00:50,967 --> 00:00:52,738
Round and round.

17
00:00:52,738 --> 00:00:55,416
She never remembers where she has been.

18
00:00:56,349 --> 00:01:01,610
Naked mole-rats know their colony by smell, so Wrinkles has a better idea.

19
00:01:01,610 --> 00:01:06,063
The first time she arrives at a chamber, she leaves a scent mark.

20
00:01:06,063 --> 00:01:09,344
In graph words, she marks the vertex.

21
00:01:09,344 --> 00:01:13,432
If she arrives at a marked vertex, she knows: been here,

22
00:01:13,432 --> 00:01:15,786
nothing new, turn around.

23
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:19,005
But a scent mark is not enough.

24
00:01:19,005 --> 00:01:22,338
At the nest, three tunnels lead out of vertex S,

25
00:01:22,338 --> 00:01:24,981
and Wrinkles can only walk down one.

26
00:01:24,981 --> 00:01:26,752
What happens to the other two?

27
00:01:26,752 --> 00:01:27,768
Pause.

28
00:01:27,768 --> 00:01:29,288
How does she remember them?

29
00:01:34,209 --> 00:01:37,191
So Wrinkles carries a pile of tokens.

30
00:01:37,191 --> 00:01:41,462
Each token reminds her of one tunnel she has not walked yet.

31
00:01:41,462 --> 00:01:44,938
It is stamped with the two chambers that tunnel connects.

32
00:01:44,938 --> 00:01:48,216
Mole-rats cannot really read, so just pretend.

33
00:01:49,136 --> 00:01:51,753
All the tokens go into one pile.

34
00:01:51,753 --> 00:01:54,097
The book calls this pile a bag.

35
00:01:54,097 --> 00:01:57,401
It is just somewhere to put things in, and take things out.

36
00:01:58,312 --> 00:02:04,198
Each token is a pair: the vertex she came from, and the vertex it leads to.

37
00:02:04,198 --> 00:02:06,880
In code, we will call these two things came_from,

38
00:02:06,880 --> 00:02:07,843
and current.

39
00:02:07,843 --> 00:02:10,848
In a few minutes, you will see why we keep both.

40
00:02:11,775 --> 00:02:14,118
The whole method is four rules.

41
00:02:14,118 --> 00:02:19,092
Rule one: start with a single token that says: came from nobody,

42
00:02:19,092 --> 00:02:20,468
leads to the nest.

43
00:02:21,344 --> 00:02:26,301
Rule two: as long as the pile is not empty, take one token out.

44
00:02:27,164 --> 00:02:31,135
Rule three: look at the vertex that token leads to.

45
00:02:31,135 --> 00:02:33,557
If it has no mark yet, mark it.

46
00:02:33,557 --> 00:02:35,328
Remember who sent her.

47
00:02:35,328 --> 00:02:36,734
That is its parent.

48
00:02:36,734 --> 00:02:40,689
Then add a new token for every edge leaving that vertex.

49
00:02:41,573 --> 00:02:46,301
Rule four: if the vertex is already marked, throw the token away.

50
00:02:46,301 --> 00:02:48,211
There is nothing new to do.

51
00:02:49,135 --> 00:02:51,336
That is the entire algorithm.

52
00:02:51,336 --> 00:02:55,672
The book calls it whatever-first search,
because we have not said which token to take

53
00:02:55,672 --> 00:02:56,817
out of the pile.

54
00:02:56,817 --> 00:02:59,565
Today, we always take the newest one.

55
00:02:59,565 --> 00:03:03,601
Next video, we will see what changes if you choose differently.

56
00:03:03,601 --> 00:03:07,924
And if you open the book, here is the translation of its letters.

57
00:03:07,924 --> 00:03:10,021
The letter s is the start.

58
00:03:10,021 --> 00:03:12,234
The bag is our token pile.

59
00:03:12,234 --> 00:03:14,448
The letter p is came_from.

60
00:03:14,448 --> 00:03:16,427
The letter v is current.

61
00:03:16,427 --> 00:03:18,363
And w is a neighbor.

62
00:03:19,276 --> 00:03:21,190
Let us run it on the colony.

63
00:03:21,190 --> 00:03:24,055
Seven vertices, twelve edges.

64
00:03:24,055 --> 00:03:29,120
Each vertex keeps a list of its neighbors, and adds tokens in list order.

65
00:03:29,120 --> 00:03:32,749
So the last neighbor in the list ends up on top of the pile.

66
00:03:33,675 --> 00:03:37,165
Start with one token: nobody to S.

67
00:03:37,165 --> 00:03:38,232
Take it out.

68
00:03:38,232 --> 00:03:40,967
S has no mark, so mark S.

69
00:03:40,967 --> 00:03:42,673
Its parent is nobody.

70
00:03:42,673 --> 00:03:48,285
S has three edges, so add three tokens: S to A, S to B,

71
00:03:48,285 --> 00:03:49,622
and S to C.

72
00:03:50,557 --> 00:03:53,031
Now the pile has three tokens.

73
00:03:53,031 --> 00:03:55,178
We always take the newest one.

74
00:03:55,178 --> 00:03:56,503
Which one is it?

75
00:04:01,418 --> 00:04:04,478
It is S to C, the last one we added.

76
00:04:04,478 --> 00:04:05,572
Take it out.

77
00:04:05,572 --> 00:04:08,267
C is new, so mark C.

78
00:04:08,267 --> 00:04:09,686
Its parent is S.

79
00:04:09,686 --> 00:04:15,568
C has four edges, to A, B, F, and back to S, so four tokens go on top.

80
00:04:16,476 --> 00:04:19,028
Newest token: C to S.

81
00:04:19,028 --> 00:04:21,398
But S already has a mark.

82
00:04:21,398 --> 00:04:22,736
Throw it away.

83
00:04:23,625 --> 00:04:25,409
Next, C to F.

84
00:04:25,409 --> 00:04:26,528
F is new.

85
00:04:26,528 --> 00:04:28,429
Mark it, parent C.

86
00:04:28,429 --> 00:04:34,142
F has three edges, to C, D, and E, so three tokens go on top.

87
00:04:35,072 --> 00:04:37,012
The newest is F to E.

88
00:04:37,012 --> 00:04:38,028
E is new.

89
00:04:38,028 --> 00:04:39,877
Mark it, parent F.

90
00:04:39,877 --> 00:04:42,702
E has edges to B, D, and F.

91
00:04:42,702 --> 00:04:44,235
Three more tokens.

92
00:04:45,151 --> 00:04:46,714
Now E to F.

93
00:04:46,714 --> 00:04:48,797
F is already marked.

94
00:04:48,797 --> 00:04:50,069
Throw it away.

95
00:04:50,980 --> 00:04:52,282
Next, E to D.

96
00:04:52,282 --> 00:04:53,558
D is new.

97
00:04:53,558 --> 00:04:55,433
Mark it, parent E.

98
00:04:55,433 --> 00:04:58,467
D has edges to A, E, and F.

99
00:04:58,467 --> 00:05:00,078
Three more tokens.

100
00:05:00,974 --> 00:05:03,474
D to F: F is marked.

101
00:05:03,474 --> 00:05:04,750
Throw it away.

102
00:05:04,750 --> 00:05:07,042
D to E: E is marked.

103
00:05:07,042 --> 00:05:08,913
Throw that one away too.

104
00:05:09,866 --> 00:05:11,155
D to A.

105
00:05:11,155 --> 00:05:14,684
A is new, so mark A, parent D.

106
00:05:14,684 --> 00:05:19,072
A has four edges, so four tokens go on top: to B,

107
00:05:19,072 --> 00:05:20,973
C, D, and S.

108
00:05:20,973 --> 00:05:23,851
Only one of those vertices is still unmarked.

109
00:05:23,851 --> 00:05:29,107
Which token will lead Wrinkles to it, and
how many will she throw away first?

110
00:05:35,036 --> 00:05:37,705
The answer: three get thrown away.

111
00:05:37,705 --> 00:05:41,169
A to S, A to D, and A to C.

112
00:05:41,169 --> 00:05:42,536
Then A to B.

113
00:05:42,536 --> 00:05:45,036
B is new, so mark B.

114
00:05:45,036 --> 00:05:46,625
Its parent is A.

115
00:05:46,625 --> 00:05:48,721
That is the seventh vertex.

116
00:05:48,721 --> 00:05:52,666
Every vertex Wrinkles can reach now has a mark.

117
00:05:52,666 --> 00:05:54,681
But the pile is not empty.

118
00:05:55,614 --> 00:06:00,731
Eleven tokens are left, and every one leads to a marked vertex.

119
00:06:00,731 --> 00:06:03,492
So rule four, over and over.

120
00:06:03,492 --> 00:06:06,004
Throw it away, throw it away.

121
00:06:06,004 --> 00:06:07,606
Watch the pile drain.

122
00:06:07,606 --> 00:06:13,323
The last two tokens, S to B and S to A, have
been waiting at the bottom since step one.

123
00:06:13,323 --> 00:06:16,695
Their vertices got marked a different way, so they are stale.

124
00:06:16,695 --> 00:06:20,054
We never dig through the pile to remove old tokens.

125
00:06:20,054 --> 00:06:22,254
We just ignore them when they come out.

126
00:06:22,254 --> 00:06:23,843
Zero tokens.

127
00:06:23,843 --> 00:06:24,999
We are done.

128
00:06:25,923 --> 00:06:31,548
The marked vertices are S, A, B, C, D, E, and F.

129
00:06:31,548 --> 00:06:33,749
X and Y never got a mark.

130
00:06:33,749 --> 00:06:38,501
That is the answer: from the nest, Wrinkles can reach seven chambers,

131
00:06:38,501 --> 00:06:41,053
and she can never reach the cut-off burrow.

132
00:06:41,053 --> 00:06:42,564
And look at the count.

133
00:06:42,564 --> 00:06:48,033
Twenty-five tokens in all: one to start,
and two for each of the twelve edges,

134
00:06:48,033 --> 00:06:49,751
one from each end.

135
00:06:49,751 --> 00:06:51,822
Only seven were useful.

136
00:06:51,822 --> 00:06:54,448
We will use that count in the next video.

137
00:06:55,341 --> 00:06:57,569
Remember the pair on each token?

138
00:06:57,569 --> 00:06:59,014
Here is why.

139
00:06:59,014 --> 00:07:02,009
Ask vertex B: who is your parent?

140
00:07:02,009 --> 00:07:02,582
A.

141
00:07:02,582 --> 00:07:03,740
Ask A?

142
00:07:03,740 --> 00:07:03,909
D.

143
00:07:03,909 --> 00:07:04,873
Ask D?

144
00:07:04,873 --> 00:07:05,720
E.

145
00:07:05,720 --> 00:07:08,376
Then F, then C, then S.

146
00:07:08,376 --> 00:07:11,575
Keep asking, and Wrinkles walks right back to the nest.

147
00:07:12,479 --> 00:07:15,214
Now draw only the parent edges.

148
00:07:15,214 --> 00:07:18,977
Six of them, one fewer than the seven vertices.

149
00:07:18,977 --> 00:07:23,208
They form a tree: one long, skinny path from the nest.

150
00:07:23,208 --> 00:07:27,128
The book says depth-first trees are usually long and skinny.

151
00:07:27,128 --> 00:07:32,176
And every marked vertex hangs from the nest by exactly one route.

152
00:07:33,118 --> 00:07:34,785
Three promises.

153
00:07:34,785 --> 00:07:36,673
First, it always stops.

154
00:07:36,673 --> 00:07:42,129
A vertex is marked only once, and only a marked vertex adds tokens.

155
00:07:42,129 --> 00:07:44,755
So the pile cannot keep growing forever.

156
00:07:45,667 --> 00:07:50,198
Second, it never marks a vertex she cannot reach.

157
00:07:50,198 --> 00:07:54,626
Every token is added by a marked vertex, along a real edge.

158
00:07:54,626 --> 00:07:59,622
So every marked vertex has a chain of parents leading back to the nest.

159
00:08:00,611 --> 00:08:04,335
Third, it marks every vertex she can reach.

160
00:08:04,335 --> 00:08:09,856
Pick any reachable vertex, call it the target, and take a shortest route to it.

161
00:08:09,856 --> 00:08:13,238
The nest, then a few vertices, then the target.

162
00:08:14,164 --> 00:08:18,682
S gets marked, so it adds a token for the next vertex on the route.

163
00:08:18,682 --> 00:08:23,826
That token cannot vanish, and the search only stops when the pile is empty,

164
00:08:23,826 --> 00:08:25,701
so the token gets taken out.

165
00:08:25,701 --> 00:08:30,439
At that moment the vertex is already marked, or it gets marked now.

166
00:08:30,439 --> 00:08:32,232
Either way, it is marked.

167
00:08:33,145 --> 00:08:35,593
Then it adds the token for the next one.

168
00:08:35,593 --> 00:08:37,976
Each vertex knocks over the next.

169
00:08:37,976 --> 00:08:41,088
Mathematicians call this induction on distance.

170
00:08:41,088 --> 00:08:45,251
It is the proof of Lemma five point one, and now you can read it.

171
00:08:46,176 --> 00:08:48,661
Here are the four rules as Python.

172
00:08:48,661 --> 00:08:50,875
Rule one is line two.

173
00:08:50,875 --> 00:08:52,841
Rule two is the while loop.

174
00:08:52,841 --> 00:08:56,033
Rule three is everything inside the if.

175
00:08:56,033 --> 00:09:00,265
And rule four is what is missing: if the vertex is already marked,

176
00:09:00,265 --> 00:09:02,956
the if is false, and nothing runs.

177
00:09:03,885 --> 00:09:05,135
Line two.

178
00:09:05,135 --> 00:09:08,677
Square brackets make a list: that is our pile.

179
00:09:08,677 --> 00:09:10,682
Inside is one pair.

180
00:09:10,682 --> 00:09:13,690
Round brackets glue two values together.

181
00:09:13,690 --> 00:09:16,580
The first is None, which means nobody.

182
00:09:16,580 --> 00:09:18,087
The second is start.

183
00:09:19,004 --> 00:09:20,423
Line six.

184
00:09:20,423 --> 00:09:24,382
Pop takes the last item out of the list: our newest token,

185
00:09:24,382 --> 00:09:25,280
a pair.

186
00:09:25,280 --> 00:09:29,421
The two names on the left of the equals sign, with a comma between them,

187
00:09:29,421 --> 00:09:30,749
split that pair.

188
00:09:30,749 --> 00:09:33,366
The first value goes into came_from.

189
00:09:33,366 --> 00:09:35,202
The second goes into current.

190
00:09:35,202 --> 00:09:37,086
That is called unpacking.

191
00:09:37,996 --> 00:09:41,824
A set remembers which vertices have a mark, and answers,

192
00:09:41,824 --> 00:09:44,012
is this one in here, very quickly.

193
00:09:44,012 --> 00:09:47,228
The curly brackets on line four make a dictionary:

194
00:09:47,228 --> 00:09:49,598
look up a vertex, get its parent.

195
00:09:49,598 --> 00:09:52,046
The loop adds one token per neighbor.

196
00:09:52,046 --> 00:09:54,220
Look at the double round brackets.

197
00:09:54,220 --> 00:09:56,824
The inner pair is the token.

198
00:09:56,824 --> 00:09:59,190
The outer ones belong to append.

199
00:10:00,136 --> 00:10:02,701
The function returns the parents table.

200
00:10:02,701 --> 00:10:07,636
Its keys are exactly the marked vertices, the answer to reachability.

201
00:10:07,636 --> 00:10:09,976
And its values are the tree.

202
00:10:10,872 --> 00:10:12,526
One more connection.

203
00:10:12,526 --> 00:10:14,987
In class you wrote build_topo_from.

204
00:10:14,987 --> 00:10:18,268
That is this same idea, written with recursion.

205
00:10:18,268 --> 00:10:22,656
There is no written pile, because Python's own call stack is the pile.

206
00:10:22,656 --> 00:10:24,909
The order of the neighbors can come out flipped,

207
00:10:24,909 --> 00:10:26,923
but the idea is the same.

208
00:10:27,813 --> 00:10:29,128
Let us retell it.

209
00:10:29,128 --> 00:10:31,485
Scent marks stop her circling.

210
00:10:31,485 --> 00:10:35,782
A pile of tokens remembers the tunnels she has not walked yet.

211
00:10:35,782 --> 00:10:38,920
And the parents form a tree back to the nest.

212
00:10:38,920 --> 00:10:43,152
Pause, and run the four rules on the graph from page one of your notes.

213
00:10:43,152 --> 00:10:46,654
Next time: what changes when the pile is a queue,

214
00:10:46,654 --> 00:10:48,356
or a priority queue.
